Q = {}
Q[1] = (f"The threshold-based approach and the direct logistic classification did <b>not</b> flag the same people. Of {R['n_test']} test applicants, {p1['mismatch']} ({f1(p1['mismatch_pct'])}%) were classified differently. The confusion matrix shows {cm_[1][1]} flagged by both rules, {cm_[0][0]} unflagged by both, zero flagged only by OLS and {p1['A_low_B_high']} flagged only by logistic regression. "
        f"The disagreement is entirely one-directional, which tells us it is a cut-off artefact rather than a disagreement about who is riskier. The two models rank applicants almost identically (Spearman {f3(p1['corr_spearman'])}, Pearson {f3(p1['corr_pearson'])}, AUC {f3(p1['auc_A'])} versus {f3(p1['auc_B'])}). "
        f"What differs is the line drawn through that ranking. Linear regression's median prediction on the test set is {f3(p1['median_ols'])}. Cutting at the median forces exactly {p1['n_flag_A']} applicants (50%) into the flagged group by construction, whatever the model learned. "
        f"The logistic rule cuts at 0.5, which is only the {f1(p1['pctile_of_05_in_ols'])}th percentile of the OLS scores, so it flags {p1['n_flag_B']} ({f1(100*p1['n_flag_B']/R['n_test'])}%). Since {pc(R['test_base_rate'])} of the test applicants were actually approved, a median split must leave at least 20 percentage points of the people who repay in the unflagged group. "
        f"The {p1['mismatch']} mismatched applicants sit between the two cut-offs: their mean logistic probability is {f3(p1['mean_pB_mismatch_A_low_B_high'])} and mean OLS score {f3(p1['mean_pA_mismatch_A_low_B_high'])}. When I cut OLS at 0.5 instead, the mismatches drop from {p1['mismatch']} to {p1['mismatch_if_ols_at_05']} ({f1(100*p1['mismatch_if_ols_at_05']/R['n_test'])}%), and OLS accuracy rises from {f3(p1['acc_A'])} to {f3(p1['acc_A_at05'])}, close to the logistic {f3(p1['acc_B'])}. "
        f"A smaller effect comes from functional form: {p1['ols_outside_01']} OLS predictions ({f1(100*p1['ols_outside_01']/R['n_test'])}%) fall outside [0, 1] (range {f3(p1['ols_min'])} to {f3(p1['ols_max'])}), whereas the logistic link bounds probabilities and bends in the tails. "
        "The practical lesson is that 'who is flagged' depends on the thresholding convention as much as on the model: a quarter of applicants change label with nothing but a rule change.")
Q[2] = (f"I split the data by sex (female n = {s21['n_A']}, male n = {s21['n_B']}) and examined the effect of age, using a compact logistic model on all 1,000 rows. The result is a significantly different effect rather than a clean Simpson reversal. "
        f"The full-model age coefficient is {f3(f_['coef'])} per SD (95% CI [{f3(f_['lo'])}, {f3(f_['hi'])}], p = {f3(f_['p'])}), a weak positive effect (odds ratio {f2(s21['age_or_per10y_full'])} per decade). For women (subgroup A) it is {f3(a_['coef'])} (CI [{f3(a_['lo'])}, {f3(a_['hi'])}], p = {f3(a_['p'])}), roughly three times larger than the pooled value (odds ratio {f2(s21['age_or_per10y_A'])} per decade). "
        f"For men (subgroup B) it is {f3(b_['coef'])} (CI [{f3(b_['lo'])}, {f3(b_['hi'])}], p = {f3(b_['p'])}): the sign flips, but the interval straddles zero, so I read it as no effect. The difference between subgroup coefficients is z = {f2(s21['z'])} (p = {f3(s21['p_diff'])}). "
        f"The pooled coefficient is a weighted blend of a strong female effect and a null male effect, so it understates the first and overstates the second. Women in the data are younger on average ({f1(s21['mean_age_A'])} vs {f1(s21['mean_age_B'])} years) and have a lower approval rate ({pc(s21['approve_rate_A'])} vs {pc(s21['approve_rate_B'])}), which is consistent with age penalising young women specifically. "
        f"I should be clear about the evidence level: I scanned {R['scan_n_comparisons']} subgroup contrasts, and after Bonferroni correction this one has p = {f2(s21['bonferroni_p'])}. It is a hypothesis worth testing, not a proven mechanism. "
        "On policy, ignoring the subgroup would lead to a single rule such as 'older applicants are marginally safer, so relax terms with age'. That rule would have no empirical basis for men and would under-correct for the real disadvantage of young female applicants. The sound conclusion is to investigate what young women's applications have in common (income, household, loan purpose) and fix that variable directly.")
Q[3] = (f"I dropped <b>loan duration</b> (months), a plausible confounder of the loan-amount effect: duration and log amount are correlated at r = {f2(R['corr_dur_logamount_train'])} in the training data, and duration's own coefficient is {f3(R['s22_duration_coef'])} (p = {f3(R['s22_duration_p'])}). The table shows the coefficient shifts (standardised units; % change = (reduced − full)/|full|).<br/>"
        f"<br/><b>Table.</b> log amount {f3(la['full'])} → {f3(la['reduced'])} ({la['pct_chg']:+.1f}%); instalment rate {f3(inst['full'])} → {f3(inst['reduced'])} ({inst['pct_chg']:+.1f}%); age {f3(age22['full'])} → {f3(age22['reduced'])} ({age22['pct_chg']:+.1f}%); number of credits {f3(nc['full'])} → {f3(nc['reduced'])} ({nc['pct_chg']:+.1f}%); present residence {f3(pr['full'])} → {f3(pr['reduced'])} ({pr['pct_chg']:+.1f}%).<br/><br/>"
        f"The log-amount coefficient shifted the most, both in absolute terms ({f3(la['abs_change'])}) and in percentage terms among coefficients of meaningful size: it more than doubles in magnitude, and its p-value moves from {f3(la['p_full'])} to below 0.001. Present residence has a large percentage change, but from a base of {f3(pr['full'])}, so that figure mostly reflects noise around zero. "
        f"Across all 44 coefficients the median absolute change is {f1(R['s22_pct_median_abs'])}%, and none changes sign. The mechanism is classic omitted-variable bias: longer loans are larger and riskier, so with duration missing the amount coefficient absorbs the duration effect. "
        f"A reviewer shown only the reduced model would conclude that loan size is a significant driver of rejection, which the full model does not support once duration is held fixed. The troubling part is that the damage is invisible to standard checks: test AUC only drops from {f3(R['s22_auc_full_test'])} to {f3(R['s22_auc_red_test'])}. "
        "The lesson is that an untested model's coefficients are conditional on whatever else happens to be included, and explanations, policy statements or fairness audits based on them are only as stable as that specification. A model that predicts adequately can still tell a misleading causal story. I therefore treat the coefficients here as descriptive associations and would require sensitivity checks of this kind before anyone cites them as reasons for a decision.")
Q[4] = ("My concrete proposal is a three-step <b>residual audit with expert review</b>, run on loan files before deployment. First, I would put the model's deviance residuals on a held-out cohort and regress them on candidate variables the model never saw, such as monthly income, employer type, region or branch, and application date. Any candidate whose coefficient is significant after correction for multiple tests is a suspected confound. "
        f"Second, I would re-fit the model with each suspect added and apply a pre-registered stability rule: if a key coefficient moves by more than 20%, flag it. This is exactly the check that exposed duration here: adding it moved log amount by {la['pct_chg']:+.1f}% and instalment rate by {inst['pct_chg']:+.1f}%. "
        "Third, I would ask two senior credit officers to review about 200 files where the model and the human decision disagree and to list what information drove their judgement independently, without seeing the model's reasons. Factors that recur and are not in the model become the next candidates. "
        f"A further hidden confound deserves explicit attention in this dataset: selection. Outcomes are observed only for applicants the bank agreed to lend to. In the training split, applicants with a critical credit history repay at {pc(crit['approve_rate_crit_train'])} against {pc(crit['approve_rate_nocrit_train'])} for the rest, a counter-intuitive result that is probably produced by who got a loan in the first place; the model's coefficient on this flag is {f3(crit['coef_crit'])}. "
        "Reject inference, or an external validation on applications from a different period or lender that includes rejected applicants, is the only way to see that confound. "
        "None of this proves that no confound remains. What it gives is a documented, repeatable process that keeps looking, with rules for what triggers an escalation, an approach more realistic than hoping that a single holdout score is enough.")
Q[5] = (f"The adversarial subset contains {s23['n_subset']} rows, not 50: only {s23['n_wrong']} test applicants ({f1(s23['pct_wrong'])}%) receive a confident wrong prediction, {s23['n_fp']} false positives (P ≥ 0.75, defaulted) and {s23['n_fn']} false negatives (P ≤ 0.25, repaid). So I used all of them. The most distinctive shared trait is the absence of the 'critical account / other credits existing' credit-history flag: {f1(crit['subset_pct_no_crit'])}% of the subset ({crit['n_no_crit_in_subset']} of {s23['n_subset']}) versus {f1(crit['test_pct_no_crit'])}% of the test set. "
        f"Among confident predictions, the error rate is {f1(R['s23_err_rate_top_trait']['err_without'])}% without the flag and {f1(R['s23_err_rate_top_trait']['err_with'])}% with it (Fisher p = {f3(crit['fisher_p_error_rate'])}), although I selected this trait post hoc from about 40 candidates. Age 25-34 is also over-represented ({f1(ab['25-34']['subset'])}% vs {f1(ab['25-34']['test'])}%). "
        f"The failure is statistical, and has two parts. First, calibration: a calibrated model would make about {f1(cal['expected_wrong_if_calibrated'])} confident errors, but Model B makes {cal['observed_wrong']}. In the P ≤ 0.25 bin, the average prediction is {f2(cal['mean_pred_lo'])} while {pc(cal['actual_rate_lo'])} of those applicants repaid. Second, additive structure: all {s23['n_fn']} false negatives have a checking balance below 200 DM, which carries coefficients of {f3(crit['coef_status_lt100'])} and {f3(crit['coef_status_0_200'])} relative to 'no checking account', so they are pushed to low probabilities, even though some repay. "
        f"The false positives average P = {f2(mech['mean_P_fp'])}, and {f1(mech['pct_nochk_or_unksav_fp'])}% have no checking account or unknown savings (coefficient {f3(crit['coef_unknown_savings'])}); the logit adds these favourable indicators on the log-odds scale without interactions to temper them. The credit-history flag is also counter-intuitive: that group repays at {pc(crit['approve_rate_crit_train'])} versus {pc(crit['approve_rate_nocrit_train'])} in training, which one additive coefficient ({f3(crit['coef_crit'])}) cannot capture. "
        "Missing interactions and selection in who received loans are the most plausible reasons the decision boundary is overconfident there.")
Q[6] = (f"The trade-off chart shows both ratios sliding away from 1 as the threshold rises: from DP {f3(sw[0.35]['DP'])} / EO {f3(sw[0.35]['EO'])} at t = 0.35 to DP {f3(sw[0.65]['DP'])} / EO {f3(sw[0.65]['EO'])} at t = 0.65. Consider two applicants from the test set, both of whom later repaid. "
        f"Applicant F is a {ex_f['age']}-year-old woman asking for DM {ex_f['amount']:,} over {ex_f['duration']} months; the model gives her P = {f2(ex_f['P'])}. Applicant M is a {ex_m['age']}-year-old man asking for DM {ex_m['amount']:,} over {ex_m['duration']} months; P = {f2(ex_m['P'])}. They are illustrative, not matched. "
        f"At t = 0.35 or 0.45 both are approved. At t = 0.55 she is rejected and he is approved. At t = 0.65 both are rejected. At t = 0.55 the group pattern is similar: {pc(sw[0.55]['tprA'])} of creditworthy women and {pc(sw[0.55]['tprB'])} of creditworthy men are approved, while the false-positive rate falls from {pc(sw[0.35]['fprA'])} to {pc(sw[0.55]['fprA'])} for women and {pc(sw[0.35]['fprB'])} to {pc(sw[0.55]['fprB'])} for men. "
        f"Prioritising predictive parity at t = 0.75 gives PP = {f3(sw[0.75]['PP'])}, so an approval means the same thing for either sex ({pc(sw[0.75]['ppvA'])} vs {pc(sw[0.75]['ppvB'])} repay). But creditworthy women are approved {pc(sw[0.75]['tprA'])} of the time against {pc(sw[0.75]['tprB'])} for men. Women who would repay lose out so that the approvals the bank does give are equally reliable. "
        f"The opposite choice hurts a different person. Going to t = 0.15 for near-perfect DP/EO means a bank lends to {pc(sw[0.15]['fprA'])} of women and {pc(sw[0.15]['fprB'])} of men who would default. "
        f"The step t = 0.75 → 0.65 raises DP by {f1(pair['DP_gain_pct'])}% but costs EO {f1(pair['EO_loss_pct'])}% (cost {f1(pair['cost'])}). Everyone gains approvals (women's TPR {pc(sw[0.75]['tprA'])} → {pc(sw[0.65]['tprA'])}, men's {pc(sw[0.75]['tprB'])} → {pc(sw[0.65]['tprB'])}), but the FPR ratio falls to {f3(sw[0.65]['FPR_ratio'])} ({pc(sw[0.65]['fprA'])} vs {pc(sw[0.65]['fprB'])}), which EO registers as a violation. With {gs['A_female']} women in the test set, such gaps are within sampling noise.")
Q[7] = (f"I would defend <b>t = 0.35</b>. At that threshold the analysis shows: demographic parity {f3(r35['DP'])} (95% CI {f2(r35['DP_lo'])}-{f2(r35['DP_hi'])}), equalized odds {f3(r35['EO'])} (CI {f2(r35['EO_lo'])}-{f2(r35['EO_hi'])}), and predictive parity {f3(r35['PP'])} (CI {f2(r35['PP_lo'])}-{f2(r35['PP_hi'])}), which is within about 1% of perfect. Overall accuracy is {f3(r35['acc'])}, the highest anywhere in the sweep. The false-positive rate is identical for the two groups ({pc(r35['fprA'])} for women and {pc(r35['fprB'])} for men, a ratio of exactly {f2(r35['FPR_ratio'])}), and precision among those approved is {pc(r35['ppvA'])} vs {pc(r35['ppvB'])}. "
        f"So at this cut-off, an approval carries the same reliability for both sexes and neither group bears more of the lender's false approvals. Moving higher raises the EO gap (EO falls to {f3(sw[0.65]['EO'])} at t = 0.65), and moving lower approves {pc(sw[0.15]['sel'])} of applicants at t = 0.15 with a false-positive rate of {pc(sw[0.15]['fprA'])} and {pc(sw[0.15]['fprB'])}, which is hardly a decision rule. "
        f"The strongest counter-argument is that the same numbers describe a lenient and unequal process. The false-positive rate is {pc(r35['fprA'])} in both groups, so two out of three eventual defaulters are approved. Equal rates of a bad outcome are not good. And the TPR gap is real: {pc(r35['tprA'])} of creditworthy women are approved against {pc(r35['tprB'])} of creditworthy men, a ratio of {f3(r35['TPR_ratio'])}, which drives the EO shortfall, and accuracy for women is {f3(r35['accA'])} against {f3(r35['accB'])} for men. "
        f"A critic could add that the DP interval ({f2(r35['DP_lo'])}-{f2(r35['DP_hi'])}) only just reaches 1, and with {gs['A_female']} women in the test set none of these ratios is precisely estimated. I accept all of this. The critic is right that t = 0.35 is a defensible compromise, not a fully fair one, and I would treat the TPR gap as a monitored harm, not as resolved. "
        f"The model also uses a sex-linked variable directly (coefficient {f3(crit['coef_female_status'])}, p ≈ 0.001), which should be reviewed before any deployment.")
Q[8] = (f"I would recommend prioritising <b>equalized odds</b> over demographic parity for credit approval. Demographic parity asks for equal approval rates whatever the groups' actual repayment rates. In this dataset those rates are not equal: {pc(s21['approve_rate_A'])} for women against {pc(s21['approve_rate_B'])} for men in the full data (and {pc(gs['pos_rate_A'])} vs {pc(gs['pos_rate_B'])} in the test set). Forcing equal approval rates would mean either rejecting repayers in one group or approving likely defaulters in the other. "
        f"Equalized odds instead asks whether people with the same true outcome are treated alike, so a creditworthy woman faces the same approval chance as a creditworthy man, which is the right idea of fairness in lending. My own numbers also show that it is the stricter measure: EO is below DP at {R['s24_EO_below_DP_count']} of {R['s24_n_thresholds']} thresholds. At t = 0.35, DP = {f3(r35['DP'])} while EO = {f3(r35['EO'])}; at t = 0.65, {f3(sw[0.65]['DP'])} against {f3(sw[0.65]['EO'])}. "
        f"The cost is demographic parity. Under EO the groups' selection rates can differ: at t = 0.35, {pc(r35['selA'])} of women are approved against {pc(r35['selB'])} of men. I think that is acceptable only when the base-rate gap is real and not itself the product of discrimination, and I cannot verify that here. "
        f"DP and EO are highly correlated across thresholds in this data (Spearman {f2(R['s24_spearman_DP_EO'])}), so the choice between them matters less than the choice of threshold. They conflict in just one adjacent pair: moving from t = {pair['t_from']:.2f} to {pair['t_to']:.2f} gains {f1(pair['DP_gain_pct'])}% in DP and loses {f1(pair['EO_loss_pct'])}% in EO, a cost of {f1(pair['cost'])} per unit gained. "
        "If the policymaker's goal is to correct historical under-lending to women, DP-style targets can be justified, but as an explicit policy choice with its own cost and not as a property of a neutral model.")
for k in range(1, 9):
    S += [P(f"Question {k}", h2), P(Q[k])]
    if k == 3:
        pass
words = {k: wc(v) for k, v in Q.items()}

# ================= PART 4
S += [PageBreak(), P("Part 4 — Reflection", h1)]
P4a = (f"<b>Direct answer.</b> Conditional deployment only: as decision support with human review, not as an automated approver. The headline reason is that accuracy ({f3(p1['acc_B'])} at 0.5) hides a {pc(r35['tprA'])} vs {pc(r35['tprB'])} approval gap for creditworthy women and men.")
P4b = (f"<b>Why a single number fails.</b> First, an overall number hides subgroup gaps. Model B's accuracy at t = 0.5 is {f3(p1['acc_B'])}, hardly better than approving everyone ({f3(sw[0.05]['acc'])}). At t = 0.35, overall accuracy is {f3(r35['acc'])} but {f3(r35['accA'])} for women and {f3(r35['accB'])} for men. A headline figure cannot tell a policymaker who bears the errors. "
       f"Second, the threshold is a values question and not a data-driven fact. Across the sweep, accuracy ranges from {f3(sw[0.95]['acc'])} to {f3(r35['acc'])}; moving the cut-off between 0.15 and 0.75 changes who is approved far more than any modelling choice. In Part 1, merely switching from a median rule to 0.5 changed the label of {p1['mismatch']} applicants ({f1(p1['mismatch_pct'])}%). No dataset tells you whether a wrongful rejection is worse than a wrongful approval. "
       f"Third, fairness metrics conflict and share no common optimum. PP is almost 1 at t = 0.75 ({f3(sw[0.75]['PP'])}) while EO is {f3(sw[0.75]['EO'])}; DP and EO come closest to 1 at t = 0.15 only because {pc(sw[0.15]['sel'])} of applicants are approved. Fourth, the model confidently fails on predictable subsets: {s23['n_wrong']} of {s23['n_test']} test applicants ({f1(s23['pct_wrong'])}%) get a confident wrong call, against about {f1(cal['expected_wrong_if_calibrated'])} expected if the model were calibrated, concentrated among applicants without the critical-history flag. "
       f"Fifth, hidden confounds remain unknowable. Dropping one variable I could name, duration, moved the amount coefficient by {la['pct_chg']:+.1f}% while AUC moved only from {f3(R['s22_auc_full_test'])} to {f3(R['s22_auc_red_test'])}; a confound I cannot name would be equally invisible. Sixth, the fairness estimates themselves are fragile: with only {gs['A_female']} women in the test set, the DP interval at t = 0.35 is {f2(r35['DP_lo'])}-{f2(r35['DP_hi'])}, so even a precise-looking ratio carries wide uncertainty. Seventh, the model uses a sex-linked attribute directly (coefficient {f3(crit['coef_female_status'])}, p ≈ 0.001), which no accuracy or AUC figure reveals. A policymaker handed one number would therefore never learn who is affected, how reliably, or how much of the apparent performance depends on choices that nobody has yet made explicit. Each of these stays invisible behind a headline figure, which is why my conditional answer carries explicit conditions: review or removal of the sex-linked variable, a documented threshold choice, subgroup monitoring, and human review of confident decisions.")
P4c = (f"<b>Grounding in evidence.</b> Mismatch: {p1['mismatch']}/{R['n_test']} ({f1(p1['mismatch_pct'])}%). Subgroup effects: age coefficient {f3(a_['coef'])} (women) vs {f3(b_['coef'])} (men). Omitted variable: log amount {la['pct_chg']:+.1f}%. Adversarial rows: {s23['n_wrong']} ({f1(s23['pct_wrong'])}%). Calibration: {cal['observed_wrong']} confident errors against {f1(cal['expected_wrong_if_calibrated'])} expected. AUC after dropping duration: {f3(R['s22_auc_full_test'])} → {f3(R['s22_auc_red_test'])}. Fairness at t = 0.35: DP {f3(r35['DP'])}, EO {f3(r35['EO'])}, PP {f3(r35['PP'])}; TPR {pc(r35['tprA'])} (women) vs {pc(r35['tprB'])} (men).")
P4d = "<b>Closing.</b> Responsible deployment means reporting these trade-offs openly: who bears which error, at what threshold, under which fairness definition, and with what uncertainty. No single metric can carry that information, and hiding the choices behind one number does not make them disappear; it only makes them unaccountable."
for t in (P4a, P4b, P4c, P4d): S += [P(t)]
wp4 = [wc(P4a), wc(P4b), wc(P4c), wc(P4d)]
print("Q word counts:", words); print("Part 4 words:", wp4, sum(wp4))

S += [P("Reproducibility", h2), P("Run the notebook top to bottom (Python 3.12; pandas, numpy, scipy, statsmodels, scikit-learn, matplotlib). It regenerates results.json, fairness_sweep.csv and all figures; build_report.py regenerates this PDF from results.json. Random seed 113 is used for the split and the bootstrap.", small)]

def footer(c, d):
    c.saveState(); c.setFont("DV", 7); c.drawString(2 * cm, 1.2 * cm, "CSET485 Assignment 1 · German Credit · roll no. ending 0113 · seed 113"); c.drawRightString(A4[0] - 2 * cm, 1.2 * cm, f"{d.page}"); c.restoreState()
doc = SimpleDocTemplate("CSET485_Assignment1_Report.pdf", pagesize=A4, leftMargin=2 * cm, rightMargin=2 * cm, topMargin=1.8 * cm, bottomMargin=2 * cm, title="CSET485 Assignment 1 - German Credit")
doc.build(S, onFirstPage=footer, onLaterPages=footer)
json.dump({"q_words": words, "p4_words": wp4}, open("wordcounts.json", "w"))
